## Why it matters beyond the missing restore

The neutering is worse than a no-op because "" is the function's success sentinel. The batch loop guards with if err_w:, so a --record run would print no warning while recording nothing — and --resume would then see LEDGER_MISSING forever and re-run the same commits every session. The entire stated purpose of the ledger, accumulation across sessions, is silently non-functional, and the docstring still asserts "Append ONE line and fsync it."

That is precisely the *"could not check" rendering as "checked; clean"* failure this file is built to refuse, shipped inside the ledger written to prevent losing verdicts.

Worth noting for the next session: build/tests PASSing is not evidence here. cargo test does not invoke this Python script's self-tests, so the green gate ran over a denominator that structurally excludes the only check that could have caught this — the same shape as the Day-179 feature-gated-test finding.

## What is genuinely sound in the diff

The design work is good and should survive the fix: parse_ledger counts malformed lines rather than dropping them; read_ledger keeps missing/unreadable/read as three distinct states with the fail-safe direction (skip nothing); baseline_from_verdict derives all seven states with COULD_NOT_CHECK → "unknown" rather than guessing; the shared CARGO_TARGET_DIR is under mkdtemp and never the repo's target/ (#832); and the anti-goals were respected — no eighth verdict state, no change to the attribution rules. The remedy is small: restore the real append_ledger body, re-run --test to green, then run the batch and write the numbers down.
